  "text": "Question eight. What's the difference between statistical and practical significance? Statistical means unlikely to be noise. Practical means big enough to matter. Hold one small effect fixed, and grow the sample. At ten users per group, the p-value is point six five. At two hundred, it slips under point oh five. At two thousand, it's essentially zero. The effect never changed. So report the effect size and its range, not just the p-value. Question nine. How would you explain a confidence interval to a stakeholder? Give a range: the new version lifts conversion by about two points, and we're confident the true lift is between one and three. The range is the estimate, plus or minus one point nine six standard errors. Ninety-five percent means that if we ran the test many times, about ninety-five intervals in a hundred would contain the true value. In this run of a hundred, four miss. And a wide interval means we need more data. How much data is the next question.",
